WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.165
The finale is for whoever
answers for AI in your organization.

2
00:00:05.165 --> 00:00:08.171
One tour of the control
room: which models run,

3
00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:13.543
what filters protect, what gets logged,
what it all costs — and the five habits this

4
00:00:13.543 --> 00:00:15.589
series has been building toward.

5
00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:18.340
You choose the machinery.

6
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.426
One gateway covers the whole catalog, or
you bring your own vendor keys — and when

7
00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:28.450
data must not leave the house, point Tale
at models running on your own hardware.

8
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.110
The workspace serves your
choice, not the other way around.

9
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.626
And who may use which
model is policy, not folklore:

10
00:00:36.626 --> 00:00:41.664
allow-lists per role and team, so the
expensive deep-reasoning model serves the

11
00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:46.510
people whose work needs it — and
nobody discovers a surprise on the invoice.

12
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:52.750
Guardrails scan both directions: what
goes into a model and what comes back.

13
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.066
Personal data can be
masked before it ever leaves,

14
00:00:56.066 --> 00:01:01.372
unsafe content blocked or flagged — and
the matched text itself is never stored.

15
00:01:01.372 --> 00:01:05.351
People paste more than they
should; the seatbelt assumes it.

16
00:01:06.551 --> 00:01:10.130
The audit log is where the
series comes full circle.

17
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:15.774
The approval you watched in episode five
is in here — who decided, what, and when.

18
00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:21.556
Every guardrail hit, every setting change,
every consequential action, attributable.

19
00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:24.241
Trust is not a feeling; it is a record.

20
00:01:25.441 --> 00:01:30.437
Usage analytics answer the question
every finance team asks in month two:

21
00:01:30.437 --> 00:01:32.559
what does this cost, and where?

22
00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:38.171
Tokens and spend by team, by model, by
agent — with budgets that turn amber before

23
00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:39.881
they turn into a problem.

24
00:01:41.081 --> 00:01:43.679
And quality gets measured, not assumed.

25
00:01:43.679 --> 00:01:49.207
Thumbs from every reply and the Arena
verdicts from episode two land here — so when

26
00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:52.937
someone asks whether the
expensive model earns its keep,

27
00:01:52.937 --> 00:01:55.401
you answer with a chart, not a hunch.

28
00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:59.233
One more dial: where the data lives.

29
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:03.839
Pin the workspace to a region
and it stays there — Switzerland,

30
00:02:03.839 --> 00:02:06.764
the EU, wherever your obligations point.

31
00:02:06.764 --> 00:02:10.201
Residency is a setting
here, not a negotiation.

32
00:02:11.401 --> 00:02:13.498
Nine episodes, five habits.

33
00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:16.450
Ground it — answers from your sources.

34
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.557
Gate it — people decide the risky steps.

35
00:02:19.557 --> 00:02:24.606
Scope it — the smallest agent,
library, and role that do the job.

36
00:02:24.606 --> 00:02:27.713
Log it — every action with a name on it.

37
00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:30.820
Measure it — cost and quality on charts.

38
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.471
That is what using AI
well actually looks like.

39
00:02:35.671 --> 00:02:38.562
That closes the main arc of the series.

40
00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:41.824
One bonus lap remains
— for your developers:

41
00:02:41.824 --> 00:02:45.308
APIs, webhooks, and
coding agents in sandboxes.

42
00:02:45.308 --> 00:02:49.311
Everyone else: the
documentation carries on from here.

43
00:02:50.511 --> 00:02:51.923
Thank you for watching.

44
00:02:51.923 --> 00:02:56.958
Everything you saw — and everything we
could not fit — lives in the documentation.

